<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Panjikent</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Penjikent, Pendzhikent</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Panjikent is a town in north-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> on the Zarafshan river, east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. Beside it lie the ruins of ancient Panjikent, a Sogdian town of the 5th to 8th centuries whose excavated houses and temples have yielded extensive wall paintings of epics, gods and banquets. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudaki">Rudaki</a> was born nearby, and his mausoleum stands in the district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/panjikent.jpg" alt="Panjikent" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bertramz · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Panjakent,RudakiMuseum.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Panjakent, also written Penjikent, lies in the Sughd province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> on the river Zeravshan. The ruins of the old town are on the outskirts of the modern city. Downstream, on the tugay-vegetated floodplain of the river, lies the Sarazm Important Bird Area. The ancient town stood about 90 km from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>, to which it was a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> state.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ancient Panjakent, known as Panchekanth, was a small but flourishing town of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sogdians">Sogdians</a> in pre-Islamic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-asia">Central Asia</a>. The Sogdians settled in city states in the Zeravshan valley and the surrounding oases, clustered mostly around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samarkand">Samarkand</a>. These states had strong rivalries, their own traditions and ruling families, and a very decentralised political system. The town grew in the 5th century, when established businessmen and landowners made their livelihoods there. In 722 Arab Muslim forces besieged and took it, and its last ruler, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divashtich">Divashtich</a>, fled into the upper Zeravshan but was captured and sentenced to death.</p><p>For around fifty years the town was ruled by new administrators, and towards the end of the 8th century the town on the upper terraces was depopulated and relocated. Records of a Penjikent rulership written in Sogdian were found not far away on Mount Mug. According to Arab geographers, in the 10th century Panjakent had a formal Friday mosque, which distinguished a town from a village; it was the easternmost city of Sogd and became well known for its walnuts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The ruins have yielded numerous murals, and many are now displayed in the Hermitage Museum and in the National Museum of Antiquities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dushanbe">Dushanbe</a>. They show details of dress and daily life, and a great variety of influences: Greek decorative styles meet the Iranian narratives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shahnameh">Shahnameh</a> and the epic cycle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostam">Rostam</a>, scenes of festivities alternate with combats, and local cults mix with Iranian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> deities. One painting shows <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> with a halo and a sacred thread, clad in a tigerskin and seated on a cushioned seat supported by two bulls, with attendants in Sogdian dress.</p><p>The production of paintings began in the 5th century and stopped in 722 with the Arab invasion, when many works were damaged or destroyed. The Russian archaeologist Boris Marshak spent more than fifty years excavating the ruins. He stayed on as director of the excavation after Tajik independence and through the civil war of 1992 to 1997, and worked with the government of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tajikistan">Tajikistan</a> to protect the site.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Panjakent” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They may have built fortified grid-plan cities at Bukhara, <b>Panjikent</b> and Herat.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hephthalite-empire">Hephthalite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Its heart was the fertile valley of the Zeravshan, which the Greeks called the Polytimetus, with the oases of Samarkand, Bukhara and <b>Panjikent</b>; to the south lay the valley of the Kashka Darya, with Kesh and Nakhshab.</blockquote><a href="/culture/sogdians">Sogdians · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Panjikent</b>, on the Zarafshan River east of Samarkand, was founded in the 5th century and grew into a walled town of merchants and landowners.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-panjikent">Principality of Panjikent</a></li></ul></section>
